## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate is integrated
against the delta time the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make the game
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Every draw the game makes runs off a generator the surface's
  `reset` seeds, above all the mine's generation: the ore scatter, the hazard and
  boulder placement, and the two material nodes' cells. The game keeps that
  generator's whole state, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces
  the same mine and the same outcome exactly. The Quantum Teleporter's height and
  speed draws are the one exception, and they are not reproducible from the seed.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The debug overlay belongs to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the surface
carries no operation for it. That layer draws the overlay and toggles it, and the
game registers what it shows.

The clock and the keyboard are the exception, because nothing outside this build
owns them. The surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it,
through `setAutoStep` and `advance`, and what puts keys down and lets them up,
through `keyDown`, `keyUp`, and `press`.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the debug overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The
engine advances the game frame by frame and a scripted clock takes it off real
time, it drives the actions the game registers so a dispatched keyboard event
moves the miner exactly as a player's key does, and it draws and toggles the debug
overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Deepcore's, under Diagnostics below.

## The driver

Deepcore has one actor, the prospector, and no operation takes it away from the
player. A scenario drives the miner with the same held actions play uses, so
walking, thrusting, and drilling in a scenario run the game's own movement and
drill code from the game's own input.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Keys reach the game through `keyDown`, `keyUp`, and `press` below. An injected key
flows through the same handling the real keyboard feeds, so it exercises the
bindings the game registers rather than a parallel path. A key left down stays
held, so a movement, thrust, or drill key drives the miner through the game's own
update across every frame `advance` runs, and it keeps driving it until `keyUp`
lets it go.

Posing the world leaves the held keys as they stand, so a caller that puts a key
down releases it itself. A `reset` releases every key.
{{else}}
Keys reach the game through the engine, which delivers a dispatched keyboard event
to the actions the game registers, so the surface carries no input operation. A
key left down stays held, so a movement, thrust, or drill key drives the miner
through the game's own update across every frame the engine advances, and it keeps
driving it until the caller releases it.

Posing the world leaves the held actions as they stand, so a caller that puts a
key down releases it itself.
{{/if}}

The two faculty gates below hold the miner's travel and its drill, and neither
touches input: a gated miner still reads the actions held, and its animation state
still follows what it is actually doing.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`DEEPCORE_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Every argument and every returned value is a plain number,
string, boolean, or plain object. Positions and velocities are in the world units
of the mine, `col` and `row` are cell coordinates in its grid, and every duration
is in seconds.

Each pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands, so a caller
that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the order it wants
them. Nothing a caller does not ask for happens.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<DeepcoreState>`, followed by the parameters this file names for it,
so `snapshot()` below is called as `snapshot(state)` and `setFuel(value)` as
`setFuel(state, value)`. A pose and a control return the next `DeepcoreState`,
built from the one they were handed, which they leave as it was; a reading returns
what it read.
{{else}}
Each operation takes the parameters this file names for it and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose and a control return nothing; a reading
returns what it read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

An argument outside the domain this file states for it is invalid, and the call
fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant. So is a call whose subject is not
in the condition the operation states, such as a cell outside the grid or a live
Core Sample where the operation requires none.

### Readings

Every reading is pure and changes nothing.

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `snapshot()` | The whole observable state, as the object under Snapshot shape below. |
| `tileAt(col, row)` | One cell's observable state, as the object under Snapshot shape below. |
| `findTile(kind)` | The `{ col, row }` of a cell of that kind, the one nearest the miner where several exist, or `null` where the mine holds none. `kind` is one of the nine tile kinds. |
| `buildings()` | One entry per surface building, `{ id, x, y, w, h }`, with `id` the building's id and `x`, `y`, `w`, `h` its footprint in world units. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

#### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

#### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's own
physics, drill, hazards, economy, and timers produce the result and the canvas
reflects it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time
and reach the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains.

`seconds` is at least `0` and `frames` is a whole number of at least `1`.
Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

### Input

| Operation | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `keyDown(code)` | Puts the key `code` down and leaves it down. `code` is a `KeyboardEvent.code`. |
| `keyUp(code)` | Lets the key `code` up. |
| `press(code)` | A `keyDown(code)` followed immediately by a `keyUp(code)`. |

Injecting a key never changes `autoStep`, and a key held across a call to
`advance` drives the miner through every frame it runs.

{{/if}}
### Restoring the world

#### `reset(options)`

Restores the whole observable state to its title-screen value: the `title` screen
with `menuIndex` at `0`, no expedition in progress, mode `standard` and world size
`standard`, an empty mine as `clearMine` leaves one, no panel open, the miner
standing on the camp ground at `SPAWN_COL` facing `east` at rest, tier `1` on
every upgrade track, a full fuel tank and a full hull, `0` Credits, an empty cargo
bay, an empty satchel, no field supplies, no rocket component installed, no live
Core Sample, no ground item, neither hazard notice fired, the camera lead at `0`,
both faculties running, and `simTime` at `0`.

`options.seed` seeds the game's generator, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).
`options` is optional and an omitted `seed` takes the default.

`muted` is untouched, because muting is a player preference rather than a value an
expedition opens with. The save slot is untouched as well, because it outlives the
session; `clearSave` is what deletes it.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

`autoStep` is untouched, because moving the clock is not posing the world. Every
held key is released.
{{/if}}

Because it restores exactly the fields above, any state of your own that a frame
depends on is derivable from them.

#### `generateMine()`

Generates a fresh mine at the current world size, drawing from the game's
generator exactly as a started expedition's own generation does, and replaces
every cell of the grid with the result. It leaves the miner, the screen, the
Credits, the tiers, the holdings, and the ground items exactly as they stand, so a
caller that wants the miner at the spawn places it itself.

#### `clearMine()`

Empties the mine: every cell of the playable columns, from `row 1` to the row above
the Core chamber, becomes an open tunnel. The bedrock border, the Core chamber and
its Core tile, and `row 0` are left as they are, so the camp and its buildings
stand. No ore, gemstone, gas pocket, lava cell, boulder, or material node
survives.

Nothing here is drilled, so nothing is banked and no gas detonates. Ground items
are untouched; `clearGroundItems` is what removes those.

#### `clearGroundItems()`

Takes every ground item off the mine at once, leaving `coreGround` `null`. A
jettisoned Core Sample removed this way is not detonated, so it damages nothing,
and the removal leaves no Sample live.

#### `clearCargo()`

Empties the cargo bay, leaving no ore and no gemstone held and the load at `0`.
Nothing is sold, so it earns no Credits.

#### `clearItems()`

Sets the held count of all six field supplies to `0`. Nothing is used, so no
item's effect fires.

### Posing the mine

`col` runs from `0` to `WORLD_COLS - 1` and `row` from `1` to `coreRow`. `row 0`
is the camp and is not posed. A cell posed to a kind keeps that kind until it is
drilled, blasted, or posed again.

| Operation | Sets |
| --- | --- |
| `setTile(col, row, kind)` | One cell's kind, to `"rock"`, `"gas"`, `"lava"`, `"stone"`, `"bedrock"`, `"tunnel"`, or `"core"`, at that band's full health where the kind is minable. It carries no ore and no material. |
| `setOreTile(col, row, ore)` | One cell to an ore vein holding `ore`, at its band's full health. `ore` is the id of one of the ten ores or three gemstones. |
| `setMaterialTile(col, row, material)` | One cell to a material node holding `material`, `"resonite"` or `"cryenite"`, at its band's full health. |
| `setTileHealth(col, row, health)` | One minable cell's remaining health, above `0` and at most that band's `BAND_HEALTH`, so a scenario can pose a partly cut tile. |
| `placeCoreSample(col, row)` | A jettisoned Core Sample on that cell, with its destabilization timer at `CORE_TIMER`. The cell is an open tunnel, and no Core Sample is live. |

A cell posed to a minable kind is posed whole: the cell it replaces is gone, along
with whatever ore, material, or health it held. Posing a tile drills nothing, so a
posed gas pocket does not detonate and a posed ore vein banks nothing.

### Posing the miner

| Operation | Sets |
| --- | --- |
| `setMinerPosition(x, y)` | The top-left corner of the miner's box, in world units. It moves the miner alone and changes no cell, so a miner placed inside a solid cell stays lodged there until the game's own collision resolves it. A scenario that needs open space opens the cells itself first. |
| `setMinerVelocity(vx, vy)` | The miner's velocity, in world units per second, positive `vy` downward. |
| `setFacing(facing)` | The miner's facing, `"east"` or `"west"`. It moves the miner nowhere. |
| `setFuel(value)` | The fuel held, from `0` to the fuel tier's maximum. |
| `setHull(value)` | The hull held, from `0` to the hull tier's maximum. A hull posed to `0` is not itself a death: the game's own continuous check is what ends the expedition, on the next update. |
| `setMinerTravel(enabled)` | Whether the miner's body moves. |
| `setMinerDrill(enabled)` | Whether the miner's drill cuts. |

#### The two faculties

Travel and the drill are on by default and a `reset` restores them. Each is held
on its own, so a scenario gives the miner exactly the faculties its subject
exercises.

With travel off, the miner's body holds the position it stands at, however long
the scenario runs and whatever is held on the keyboard: gravity, walking, thrust,
knockback, and collision displacement all move it nowhere, and its velocity stays
where it was posed. Everything else about it carries on. It still reads as
grounded from the cells beneath its box, so it still starts and holds a cut; the
drill still spends fuel; life support still burns; lava contact, a gas blast, and
a Core detonation still cost hull; and the camera still follows it.

With the drill off, no cut starts and none progresses, so no cell loses health, no
cell breaks, nothing is banked, and no drill hit spends fuel. Everything else
carries on: the miner walks, falls, thrusts, and takes damage exactly as it does
with the drill running, and the animation state follows what it is actually doing.

### Posing the expedition

| Operation | Sets |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(screen)` | The current screen, to one of the eight screen names. It changes no other field, so the summary a `victory` or `game-over` screen shows is the one the expedition has accumulated. |
| `setPanel(panel)` | The open panel, to one of the six panel ids, or `null` to close whatever is open. A panel opens wherever the miner stands. |
| `setMenuIndex(index)` | The highlighted item on the current screen, counted from `0`, within the range that screen's menu holds. |
| `setMode(mode)` | The expedition's mode, `"standard"` or `"hardcore"`. |
| `setWorldSize(size)` | The expedition's world size, `"quick"`, `"standard"`, or `"marathon"`, and with it `coreRow`. It changes no cell, so a caller that wants terrain at the new depth follows it with `generateMine` or `clearMine`. |
| `setCredits(value)` | The Credits balance, at least `0`. |
| `setTier(track, tier)` | One upgrade track's tier, from `1` to the track's highest. `track` is one of the seven track names. Everything the tier defines follows from it, and the fuel and hull held are clamped to the new maxima and otherwise untouched. It costs no Credits. |
| `setCargo(ore, count)` | The number of units of one ore or gemstone in the cargo bay, at least `0`, ignoring the slot cap so a scenario can pose any load. |
| `setMaterial(material, count)` | The number of one exotic material in the satchel, `"resonite"` or `"cryenite"`, at least `0`. |
| `setCoreCarried(carried)` | Whether a Core Sample is in the satchel. `setCoreCarried(true)` puts one there with its timer at `CORE_TIMER` and requires that no Core Sample is live; `setCoreCarried(false)` removes it and its timer with it, without detonating it. |
| `setCoreTimer(seconds)` | The seconds left on the live Core Sample's timer, above `0`. It requires a live Core Sample, carried or jettisoned. |
| `setItemCount(item, count)` | The number of one field supply held, by its id, at least `0`. |
| `setRocketInstalled(count)` | How many rocket components are installed, `0` through `5`, taken in the order the checklist builds them. It costs no Credits and consumes no material. |
| `setNoticeFired(hazard, fired)` | Whether the one-time notice for `"gas"` or `"lava"` has already been raised this expedition. It raises no card and dismisses none. |
| `setCameraLead(lead)` | The camera's carried vertical lead, in world units, positive downward, within `[-CAM_LEAD_MAX, CAM_LEAD_MAX]`. The camera moves to match on the next update. |
| `clearSave()` | Deletes the save slot, leaving `hasSave` `false`. |
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
| `setMuted(muted)` | The audio mute toggle, the same one the mute control flips. |
{{/if}}

### The controls

These are the named counterparts of the on-screen controls a player clicks. Each
runs the game's own rule for that control, on the game as it stands, so none of
them poses an outcome. A control the game's own rules refuse changes nothing and
shows the note it would show for a player, exactly as the control it stands for
would.

| Operation | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `dropOre(ore)` | Discards one unit of that ore from the cargo bay, as the inventory's drop control does. The unit is lost. |
| `sell()` | Sells the whole cargo at the Ore Market. |
| `buyFuel()` | Buys `FUEL_BUY_INCREMENT` units of fuel at the Fuel Depot. |
| `fillFuel()` | Buys fuel to full at the Fuel Depot, paying only for what is missing and only as far as the Credits reach. |
| `buyRepair()` | Buys `REPAIR_BUY_INCREMENT` points of hull repair at the Fuel Depot. |
| `repairFull()` | Buys hull repair to full at the Fuel Depot, on the same terms as `fillFuel`. |
| `buyUpgrade(track)` | Buys the next tier on that track at the Upgrade Shop. |
| `buyItem(item)` | Buys one field supply of that id at the Supply Depot. |
| `useItem(item)` | Uses one field supply of that id, as its hotkey and its inventory `USE` control do. |
| `jettison()` | Drops a carried Core Sample onto the miner's cell as a ground item, as the jettison control does. |
| `fabricate()` | Fabricates the next uninstalled rocket component at the Launch Pad. |
| `launch()` | Launches, as the Launch Pad's `LAUNCH` control does. |
| `save()` | Saves the expedition, as activating the Save Pad does. |
| `dismissNotice()` | Dismisses the hazard notice card on screen, as a click on it does. |

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "mode-select" | "size-select" | "how-to-play"
        | "in-mine" | "paused" | "victory" | "game-over",
  panel: null | "fuel-depot" | "ore-market" | "upgrade-shop"
       | "supply-depot" | "launch-pad" | "inventory",
  mode: "standard" | "hardcore",
  worldSize: "quick" | "standard" | "marathon",
  coreRow: <number>,            // the deepest row at the current world size
  menuIndex: <number>,          // the screen's highlighted menu item, from 0
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,          // false while the caller clocks the game
{{/if}}
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,            // accumulated game time, in seconds
  hasSave: <boolean>,
  credits: <number>,
  creditsEarned: <number>,      // the running total earned this expedition
  depthMeters: <number>,
  deepestDepthMeters: <number>,
  coreTimer: <number> | null,   // seconds left on a live Core Sample, else null
  coreGround: null | { col: <number>, row: <number> },
  camera: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,   // the world point drawn at the viewport corner
    lead: <number>,             // the carried vertical lead, positive downward
  },
  miner: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,   // the top-left of the miner's box, in world units
    vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
    col: <number>, row: <number>,
    facing: "east" | "west",
    state: "idle" | "walk" | "drill-down" | "drill-side"
         | "jetpack" | "fall" | "hurt" | "fuel-out",
    grounded: <boolean>,
    travel: <boolean>, drill: <boolean>,   // the two faculties
    fuel: <number>, maxFuel: <number>,
    hull: <number>, maxHull: <number>,
    overloaded: <boolean>,
    drilling: null | { col: <number>, row: <number>,
                       dir: "down" | "left" | "right", progress: <number> },
  },
  cargo: {
    slotsUsed: <number>, slotCap: <number>,
    loadKg: <number>,
    liftLimitKg: <number>,
    ore: { <oreId>: <count> },   // held ore and gemstones, nonzero entries only
  },
  satchel: {
    resonite: <number>, cryenite: <number>,
    coreSample: <boolean>,
  },
  tiers: {
    fuel: <number>, drill: <number>, cargo: <number>, hull: <number>,
    jetpack: <number>, radiator: <number>, scanner: <number>,
  },
  items: {
    "dynamite": <number>, "plastic-explosives": <number>,
    "quantum-teleporter": <number>, "matter-transmitter": <number>,
    "nanobots": <number>, "emergency-fuel": <number>,
  },
  rocket: {
    installed: [ <componentId> ],       // in order: "hull-frame", "fuel-cells",
                                        // "guidance", "thruster", "ignition"
    nextComponent: <componentId> | null,
  },
  scanner: {
    locked: <boolean>,
    target: "resonite" | "cryenite" | null,
    dirX: <number>, dirY: <number>,     // a unit direction to the target
    distanceTiles: <number> | null,
  },
  notice: null | { hazard: "gas" | "lava", shown: <boolean> },
  noticesFired: { gas: <boolean>, lava: <boolean> },
  summary: null | {
    deepestDepthMeters: <number>, creditsEarned: <number>,
    elapsedSeconds: <number>, mode: "standard" | "hardcore",
    componentsInstalled: <number>,
    deathCause: "fuel-out" | "hull-destroyed" | "core-detonation" | null,
  },
}
```

An `<oreId>` is the id of an ore or gemstone, its name in lower case, so Ferron is
`"ferron"` and Roselite is `"roselite"`.

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. A field the current
screen does not use reports its resting value rather than going missing: `summary`
is `null` until the expedition ends, `coreTimer` and `coreGround` are `null` while
no Sample is live, `panel` is `null` while no panel is open, `notice` is `null`
while no card is armed or on screen, and `menuIndex` rests at `0` on `in-mine`.

Several fields are derived rather than stored: `depthMeters` and
`deepestDepthMeters` from the miner's position, `overloaded` from `loadKg` against
`liftLimitKg`, and `drilling.progress`, which runs `0` to `1` as the target cell's
health drains. `scanner.locked` is `false`, with the direction zero and the
distance `null`, whenever nothing needed is in range, matching the indicator being
hidden. `notice.shown` is `true` only while the card is drawn, so it is `false`
over the `NOTICE_DELAY` between the hit and the card appearing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
Three of the snapshot's figures are live reads rather than kept copies: `muted` is
the engine's own mute bit, read from the audio bus at the call, `camera.x` and
`camera.y` are the engine camera's own position, and `simTime` is the frame
clock's accumulated simulated time.
{{else}}
The game refreshes the following every frame:

- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit. Refresh it from the runtime in every
  update, since the runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of
  it.
- `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every update, whatever the screen.
{{/if}}

### What `tileAt` returns

The grid is large, so it is read a cell at a time rather than dumped whole.
`tileAt(col, row)` returns:

```js
{
  kind: "rock" | "ore" | "material" | "gas" | "lava"
      | "stone" | "bedrock" | "tunnel" | "core",
  band: "topsoil" | "rockbed" | "deepstone" | "coreshell" | null,
  ore: <oreId> | null,
  material: "resonite" | "cryenite" | null,
  health: <number> | null,
  maxHealth: <number> | null,
}
```

`band` is `null` for a cell outside the four bands. `ore` is set on an ore cell and
`material` on a material node, and both are `null` otherwise. `health` and
`maxHealth` are `null` for a cell that is not minable. A cell outside the grid
reads as `bedrock` with every other field `null`.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only. It shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources, it draws over the running game without changing anything, and
it is hidden until it is toggled. Register at least the current screen and panel,
the mode and world size, the miner's position, velocity, state, facing, and
grounded flag, its fuel and hull against their maxima, any active drill target and
its progress, the Credits, the depth in meters, the cargo's slots and load with
the overload flag, the satchel's contents, the seven upgrade tiers, the Core
Sample's timer, and the scanner's lock and target. Those are the same facts the
snapshot reports. Keep each source short enough to read on a line, and keep every
one a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources plainly and clearly apart from the status bar, the backtick key
(`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides it, and it starts hidden. It
reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Deepcore's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<DeepcoreState>`, and reports the value that state carries.
Drawing the panel, toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are
the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Deepcore's part, through the diagnostics
registries the engine provides. Each source is a function of no arguments, called
at each read, reporting the live game at the moment it runs. Drawing the panel,
toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
